    path: server, accounts, auth, a database, an event log, delivery infrastructure. Weeks, not days.
  - [1mTime[0m — due dates, overdue tasks, "this is due tomorrow." No other users, no accounts, no event log. This is
    [3mreminders[0m, and it's maybe a tenth of the work. It's also what most people mean when they say they want to be
    notified about their tasks.
  - [1mAn external system[0m — something syncing tasks in from elsewhere. Depends entirely on what that thing is.

  I want to name the tradeoff plainly: if the answer is "time," then the multi-user notification architecture is
  entirely wasted work, and picking it now costs you a backend you never needed. If the answer is "other people," then
  starting with a single-user local app means the storage layer gets rewritten when accounts arrive — worth knowing up
  front even though we'd still build tasks first.

  [1mMy recommendation regardless of your answer: sub-project one is tasks + persistence.[0m Every path above needs it, it's
  the cheapest thing to get right, and it's the only piece I can design without speculating. We'd brainstorm that
  properly now — its own spec, plan, implementation — and notifications becomes its own cycle once there's something
  real to notify about.

  But I need the source-of-change answer before I even scope sub-project one, because it decides whether storage is a
  local browser store or a server from day one.
